What it responds to: a word that the tokenizer split into pieces, where the current piece is
an English derivational suffix that closes the word, above all the adverb ending "-ly"
(pieces "ly", "ely", "ally", "ily", "ably", "ively", "fully", e.g. "night|ly", "Weather|ly"),
then the noun endings "-ity" / "-ality", "-ance" / "-ence", "-ment" / "-ement" (e.g.
"special|ity", "assist|ance"). A bare carriage return "\\r" (terminal progress bars) also
fires. Everything else, including whole words, is near zero.

The program is stateless: it looks only at the newest token. Values are the average
activation of each class, measured on training text. The component's sign varies a lot
within each class, so this explains only a few percent of its variance (about 3% on eval);
most of the variance is token-specific and does not reduce to a concept.
"""

READS = ()

LY_PIECES = {"ly", "ely", "ally", "ily", "ably", "ively", "fully"}
NOUN_PIECES = {"ity", "ality", "ance", "ence", "ment", "ement", "lement"}


def step(carry, prefix, upstream):
    piece = prefix.token_strings[-1]
    if piece == "\r":
        value = 0.5
    elif piece.isascii() and piece.isalpha() and piece.islower() and not piece[:1].isspace():
